<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">interrupts</ansi>

A spell takes time: its caster <ansi fg="yellow">chants</ansi> for a round or two before it goes
off (<ansi fg="command">help cast</ansi>). A chant is fragile. <ansi fg="yellow">A weapon blow that draws blood
may break it</ansi>:

  <ansi fg="command">The goblin hexer's chant breaks off under the blow. (Withering Hex interrupted)</ansi>

- Heavy force <ansi fg="yellow">always breaks</ansi> a chant: a <ansi fg="yellow">critical hit</ansi> that gets through
  the armor, or a blow that <ansi fg="yellow">staggers</ansi>, <ansi fg="yellow">knocks down</ansi>, or <ansi fg="yellow">stuns</ansi> the caster.
- Any other blow breaks it by chance, more often the harder it lands
  against the caster's maximum health: a scratch on a sturdy caster breaks
  it about <ansi fg="yellow">40%</ansi> of the time, and a blow that takes <ansi fg="yellow">a quarter of their health</ansi>
  (or more) <ansi fg="yellow">90%</ansi>. The chance is 40% plus 2% for each percent of the
  caster's maximum health the blow took, so a frail healer's chant breaks
  more easily than a sturdy one's.
- When the chant holds, the caster flinches and chants on:
  <ansi fg="command">Brother Oswin flinches, but the chant holds. (Minor Heal, chant held)</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">You flinch, but your chant holds. (Minor Heal, chant held)</ansi>

A melee blow, a sling stone, or an arrow can break it, and so can a blow
you take in someone else's place (guarding them, or catching it in the
front row). A miss, a blow blocked, parried, or dodged, a hit that does no
damage, bleeding or burning, and a spell never do.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">When your chant breaks</ansi>

- The spell is lost, and <ansi fg="yellow">half its mana comes back</ansi> (rounded down):
  <ansi fg="command">The blow breaks your chant, and Minor Heal is lost. (Minor Heal interrupted, 1 mana back)</ansi>
- The same goes for your companions. A healer or caster tries again at its
  next chance, as its strategy says (<ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">When a foe's chant breaks</ansi>

- An enemy caster, such as the <ansi fg="yellow">goblin hexer</ansi> of the Dark Forest, starts
  the chant again from the first word at its next turn. It spends no more
  mana, but must chant the whole spell again, and every blow that draws
  blood may break it again.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Keeping your casters chanting</ansi>

- Put healers and casters in the <ansi fg="yellow">back row</ansi>, where most foes' blows can't
  reach them (<ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>).
- Give them a <ansi fg="yellow">guardian</ansi>: a guarded blow lands on the guardian instead
  (<ansi fg="command">help guardian</ansi>).
- Break the enemy's chants: the <ansi fg="command">casters</ansi> focus turns your whole company on
  foes that chant (<ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>). A foe chanting from its back row can
  still be reached with a bow or sling.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Wind-ups</ansi>

Some big foes wind up a heavy blow in plain view before they swing. The
<ansi fg="yellow">forest ogre</ansi> of the Dark Forest winds up <ansi fg="yellow">Crushing Blow</ansi>:

  <ansi fg="command">The forest ogre plants his feet and drags his great club up over his shoulder, eyes on Tamsin Reed. (winding up: Crushing Blow, 1 round)</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">The forest ogre brings his great club down with all his weight.</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">The forest ogre's great club catches Tamsin Reed across the jaw. (Crushing Blow, 18 damage, knocked down)</ansi>

- Winding up takes the foe's whole turn. At its <ansi fg="yellow">next turn</ansi> it swings once
  at the one it named, with <ansi fg="yellow">double damage</ansi> before armor, and a blow that
  gets through the armor <ansi fg="yellow">knocks the target down</ansi> (they lose their next
  action; <ansi fg="command">help statuses</ansi>). It can still miss, or be blocked, parried,
  or dodged (<ansi fg="command">help defense</ansi>).
- Only <ansi fg="yellow">heavy force</ansi> breaks a wind-up: a <ansi fg="yellow">critical hit</ansi> that gets through the
  armor, or a blow that <ansi fg="yellow">staggers</ansi>, <ansi fg="yellow">knocks down</ansi>, or <ansi fg="yellow">stuns</ansi> the foe:
  <ansi fg="command">The forest ogre staggers, and the blow dies before it can fall. (Crushing Blow interrupted)</ansi>
  An ordinary blow never breaks a wind-up, however hard it lands, and
  neither does a shield bash. A spell's damage never breaks one either,
  but anything that stuns or knocks it down (a spell too) costs it the
  turn, and the wind-up with it.
- If the one it named has gone, the blow falls on whoever it is aiming at
  now. With no one left in reach it is wasted:
  <ansi fg="command">The forest ogre lets his great club fall, with nothing left to strike. (Crushing Blow wasted)</ansi>
- A broken wind-up is lost. After a wind-up lands or breaks, the foe
  swings normally for <ansi fg="yellow">two combat rounds</ansi> before it can wind up again.
- To weather it: hit the foe with everything while it winds up (every
  blow is a chance at a critical hit), keep your frail members out of its
  reach (<ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>), and give its target a <ansi fg="yellow">guardian</ansi>, who steps
  in and takes the blow (<ansi fg="command">help guardian</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Shield counters</ansi>

When someone holding a <ansi fg="yellow">shield</ansi> blocks a melee blow (<ansi fg="command">help defense</ansi>), they
may slam the shield back into the attacker:

  <ansi fg="command">Tamsin Reed catches the bandit captain's blow on her shield.</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">Tamsin Reed drives her shield back into the bandit captain. (shield bash, 3 damage, stunned)</ansi>

- A blocked blow gives a <ansi fg="yellow">{{ config "GamePlay.Combat.BashChanceMin" }}% to {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.BashChanceMax" }}%</ansi> chance, more the stronger and
  more skilled the bearer is than the attacker (<ansi fg="command">help evasion</ansi>).
  A plain miss is never countered. The bash does <ansi fg="yellow">1 to 4 damage</ansi>, and one
  bash in four also <ansi fg="yellow">stuns</ansi> the attacker (<ansi fg="command">help statuses</ansi>).
- At most <ansi fg="yellow">one counter a round</ansi> for each shield-bearer.
- No counter against a bow or sling, and none while the bearer is knocked
  down, stunned, or chanting (a stunned bearer can't block at all).
- It works both ways: a foe with a shield counters you. You choose nothing;
  holding a shield is enough.
- A bash is a counter strike only: it breaks no chant and no wind-up.

The battle summary lists the chants and wind-ups each side broke, and how
many of your blows a foe's chant withstood ("failed") (<ansi fg="command">help battle-summary</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help cast</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help guardian</ansi>.

Wind-up preparation and release each occupy a whole combat round; tempo
cannot prepare and release a blow in the same round (<ansi fg="command">help tempo</ansi>).
